    "summary": "Jam‑filled Saint Stephen, crisp acoustic set and strong Easy Wind",
    "blurb": "The show opens with a clean acoustic set highlighted by Garcia’s crystal‑clear vocals on “I Know You Rider” and sweet harmonies from Weir. The electric second half delivers high‑energy jams – especially “Easy Wind”, “Hard To Handle” and the soaring “It’s A Man’s World”. The encore finishes with a fiery “Saint Stephen” segue into “Casey Jones”.",
    "review": "June 5, 1970 at Fillmore West captures the Grateful Dead in fine form during their celebrated San Francisco run. An intimate acoustic opening showcases Garcia’s voice in top‑shape on “I Know You Rider”, while Bob Weir’s harmonies add sweetness to the early numbers. The band then lifts off into a vigorous electric set; “Easy Wind” and “Hard To Handle” burn with tight improvisation, and “It’s A Man’s World” stands out as a soulful showcase for both vocals and Pigpen’s organ. Highlights continue with a jam‑filled “Saint Stephen” that rolls straight into an exuberant “Casey Jones”. Throughout the night Mickey Hart endures a good‑natured heckle, adding humor to an otherwise tight performance, and Bill Kreutzmann locks in solidly behind him. Despite occasional patchy patches in the recording, the show’s energy and musicianship hold up strongly.",
